---
/* The clip shapes of the design, defined once per page.
 *
 * A `clip-path: url(#…)` resolves only against a definition in the *same*
 * document, so these travel with the layout rather than with whoever uses them:
 * forgetting them does not fail a build, it publishes an unclipped photo. A
 * guard checks that every reference in a published page finds its shape here.
 *
 * The geometry is generated — src/lib/shapes.ts, which is where the reasoning
 * about it lives — and the names are Material 3's:
 *
 *   f-quadrifoglio             → clip-clover-4      (4-leaf clover)
 *   f-esafoglio                → clip-cookie-6      (6-sided cookie)
 *   f-ottofoglio, m-ottofoglio → clip-clover-8      (8-leaf clover)
 *   f-gemma                    → clip-gem           (gem)
 *   —                          → clip-pill          (pill)
 *   f-obliqua                  → clip-skewed        (no Material equivalent)
 *
 * `clip-pill` is in no design file: it is Material's own, added in PR 6.
 *
 * The last one is the exception, and it is named plainly for a reason:
 * Material's `slanted` is a rounded square on a tilted axis, and this is a
 * hard-edged quadrilateral with two sloping sides. Borrowing the name would
 * have promised a shape this is not — the whole argument for taking the names
 * from Material was that they say what you get. It is also the one shape that
 * kept the export's own geometry, for the same reason.
 *
 * `clip-pill` is not the pill the design uses. That one is
 * `border-radius: var(--radius-pill)` on buttons and labels, it is used seven
 * times, and it could not be a clip path at all: under `objectBoundingBox` the
 * radii are fractions of width and height, so `rx=.5 ry=.5` gives an ellipse
 * rather than a capsule. Material's Pill is a rounded, tilted quadrilateral —
 * a different geometry that happens to share the name, and not a substitute
 * for the radius.
 *
 * The eight-lobed shape appears once and not twice: the export defines it in
 * both files because it is two designs — desktop and mobile — and this is one
 * site.
 *
 * The design applies only the eight-lobed one, to the 56×56 portraits of the
 * guests, and so does this site — `GuestRow`. The others are shown in the
 * gallery at /componenti, which is what keeps them from being published for
 * PRs on end with nobody able to see whether they are right.
 *
 * The `<svg>` is zero-sized and hidden from assistive technology: it defines,
 * it does not draw.
 */
import { CLIP_SHAPES } from '../lib/shapes.ts';
---

<svg width="0" height="0" style="position: absolute" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
  <defs>
    {
      CLIP_SHAPES.map((shape) => (
        <clipPath id={shape.id} clipPathUnits="objectBoundingBox">
          <path d={shape.path} />
        </clipPath>
      ))
    }
  </defs>
</svg>
